  "content": "<b>Meaning/Usage</b>: Used to tell someone to get in the car\n<b>Explanation</b>: \"Hop\" is like a small jump. So this phrase is pretty straightforward.\n\"<b>Hop in</b>. You can ride with me to the grocery store.\"\n\"<b>Hop in</b>. We need to leave right away.\"\n\"If you need a ride home, <b>hop on in</b>.\"\n<b>A.</b> \"I can't believe my car broke down again.\"\n<b>B.</b> \"<b>Hop in</b>, and I'll give you a ride home.\"\n<b>A.</b> \"Thanks man.\"\n<b>B.</b> \"You're welcome.\"\nOther Common Sentences\n\"Get in the car.\"\n\"Jump in the car.\"",
